ChangeThreadPriority: answer the thread's previous priority, not the success code (journal 005)

What it fixes: ps2_syscalls::ChangeThreadPriority and iChangeThreadPriority
(ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp, changePriorityImpl) answered KE_OK (0) whenever the
change succeeded, discarding the previous priority that EeScheduler::changePriority already produces
in its oldPriority reference parameter. The EE kernel answers the priority the thread had (a negative
error code only on failure), and the game's code depends on it: its CRI library raises the calling
thread to priority 1 on entering a critical section (0x314320: the answer is kept in DAT_003d9c10)
and restores the priority from that kept value on leaving it (0x3143a8:
ChangeThreadPriority(GetThreadId(), DAT_003d9c10)) — both measured with tools/re/decompile.sh.
With 0 as the answer, the restore asked for priority 0, which EeScheduler::changePriority rejects with
KE_ILLEGAL_PRIORITY (-403) (EeScheduler.cpp:704), so the main thread stayed at the 1 the entry had
set instead of returning to 0x18 (24) that 0x314FF0 had given it. At priority 1 it outranks the four
CRI threads (8, 16, 18, 25), this scheduler never preempts a running thread for a lower-priority one
(checkpointDue -> hasReadyAtOrAbovePriority, EeScheduler.cpp:379-385, :2089-2100), so the threads that
drive the ADXF device state machine (0x31B1F0) never ran, the file state byte handle+1 stayed 2 and
the boot stayed in the poll loop at 0x331C60 (journal 005).

How it was verified: tests/runtime/change_priority_return_test.cpp (new) calls the handlers the way
the generated code does and runs the game's own sequence with the values measured in the run: the
calling thread at 24 (what 0x314FF0 sets), the raise to 1 with its answer kept, the restore with that
answer, and then an illegal priority (0) and an unknown thread id to show that errors are still
errors. Against the tree without this patch the test fails: "the raise answers the priority the
thread had (24), got 0", "the restore answers ... got -403", "the thread ends at the priority it had
(24), got 1", "the rejected call changed nothing, got 1", "iChangeThreadPriority answers the previous
priority (8), got 0". With the patch all checks pass, and the other seven executables of
tests/runtime (change_priority_return, host_gs_hook, host_loop, host_pad_source, host_vu1_hook,
init_thread_priority, iop_host_spu2, module_buffer_load) pass built with PS2X_WITH_RAYLIB=OFF and
DISPLAY unset. In the game (bash tools/run/smoke.sh 60, headless, the port's host program) the guest
thread table (DON_TRACE_THREADS=1) shows the fix took effect: the main thread ends at priority 24 and
the CRI threads run (journal 005's "Verification" and journal 006).

Origin: original work for this project (T-016). The console behaviour is taken from the game's own
code (0x314320, 0x3143a8, 0x314FF0, tools/re/decompile.sh) and from the scheduler's existing
oldPriority out parameter.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
index d1f4dd6..51151a7 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
@@ -116,7 +116,12 @@ namespace ps2_syscalls
             const int priority = static_cast<int>(getRegU32(ctx, 5));
             int oldPriority = 0;
             const int result = ee.changePriority(id, priority, interruptSafe, oldPriority);
-            setReturnS32(ctx, result);
+            // The EE kernel answers the priority the thread had, not a success code: the game's CRI
+            // library raises its own thread to priority 1 in a critical section and restores the
+            // priority from this answer (0x314320/0x3143a8). With KE_OK the restore passed 0, which
+            // changePriority rejects, and the main thread was left at 1, starving the CRI threads
+            // (journal 005). On failure the error code is the answer, as in the kernel.
+            setReturnS32(ctx, result == KE_OK ? oldPriority : result);
             ee.transferIfRequested(interruptSafe);
         }
 
